WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.920
The first film used one genome to supply what another lacked.

2
00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:08.240
Can a supplied compound reveal where a pathway is blocked?

3
00:00:09.560 --> 00:00:12.700
Watch one cell build a needed product through a chain of steps.

4
00:00:13.783 --> 00:00:16.303
Each arrow needs a gene-controlled function.

5
00:00:17.563 --> 00:00:21.463
A damaging mutation can remove one function and stop the flow.

6
00:00:22.743 --> 00:00:27.403
The cell then lacks the final product, even though every step after the block may

7
00:00:27.403 --> 00:00:28.403
still work.

8
00:00:28.983 --> 00:00:32.183
A nutritional screen makes that hidden block visible.

9
00:00:33.523 --> 00:00:37.643
Keep each mutant alive on complete medium, then test minimal medium.

10
00:00:38.983 --> 00:00:44.283
Adding candidate compounds separately asks which supplied material can restore the

11
00:00:44.283 --> 00:00:45.663
missing synthesis and growth.

12
00:00:46.767 --> 00:00:48.567
Add an upstream intermediate.

13
00:00:49.907 --> 00:00:53.507
It enters before the broken arrow and reaches the same barrier.

14
00:00:54.847 --> 00:00:56.187
Nothing arrives downstream.

15
00:00:57.567 --> 00:00:59.067
The growth gauge stays dark.

16
00:01:00.627 --> 00:01:04.647
Failure to rescue tells us only that this supply did not bypass the block.

17
00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:07.620
Now add a downstream intermediate.

18
00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:10.820
It enters beyond the broken arrow.

19
00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:16.300
The later functions convert it into the needed product, and growth returns.

20
00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:20.900
Rescue places the block somewhere before that entry point.

21
00:01:21.983 --> 00:01:24.123
A new mutant blocks the last arrow.

22
00:01:25.403 --> 00:01:29.723
Which supply can restore growth: the early intermediate, or the final product?

23
00:01:31.183 --> 00:01:32.583
Trace each route before choosing.

24
00:01:38.650 --> 00:01:42.950
The early intermediate still meets the last block, so it cannot rescue.

25
00:01:44.230 --> 00:01:47.150
The final product needs no remaining conversion.

26
00:01:48.610 --> 00:01:49.810
Supplying it restores growth.

27
00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:54.030
The useful supply entered after every blocked step.

28
00:01:55.083 --> 00:01:56.523
Compare several mutants.

29
00:01:57.843 --> 00:02:00.583
An early block is rescued by many later supplies.

30
00:02:01.943 --> 00:02:03.703
A middle block has fewer options.

31
00:02:04.983 --> 00:02:07.163
A late block may accept only the product.

32
00:02:08.563 --> 00:02:11.943
Nested rescue patterns let us read pathway order backward.

33
00:02:13.067 --> 00:02:14.787
This reading needs assumptions.

34
00:02:16.207 --> 00:02:19.667
The supplied compound must enter the cell and join the pathway.

35
00:02:20.927 --> 00:02:23.447
The pathway must behave like the tested chain.

36
00:02:24.687 --> 00:02:29.567
A rescue class can contain several genes, because different functions may act

37
00:02:29.567 --> 00:02:30.727
between the same supplies.

38
00:02:31.950 --> 00:02:34.170
A strain can carry more than one block.

39
00:02:35.410 --> 00:02:39.670
An early supply may bypass the first barrier yet stop at a later one.

40
00:02:41.210 --> 00:02:45.890
Growth returns only when the supplied material enters after every remaining block.

41
00:02:46.933 --> 00:02:52.153
Complementation and feeding experiments ask related rescue questions, but their

42
00:02:52.153 --> 00:02:57.553
groups differ. Complementation groups sort mutations by shared function.

43
00:02:59.013 --> 00:03:01.553
Rescue classes sort blocks by pathway position.

44
00:03:02.913 --> 00:03:07.453
Keeping those readouts separate prepares us to compare function with recombination.

45
00:03:08.617 --> 00:03:14.137
Rescue narrows the possibilities; it does not expose the broken reaction directly.

46
00:03:15.537 --> 00:03:20.337
Measure accumulated compounds or test more intermediates when neighboring steps

47
00:03:20.337 --> 00:03:21.217
remain possible.
